\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


For a positive-definite form $Q(x)=x^TAx$ on $\mathbb R^n$, $S(Q)$ contains the nonzero integer vectors minimizing $Q$. A form is well rounded when $S(Q)$ spans $\mathbb R^n$. With the minimum fixed, the cell $C_S$ consists of forms having precisely $S$ as their minimal-vector set; classes are taken up to integral invertible changes of basis. Its Bacher matrix is $\sum_{x\in S}xx^T$. Weak eutaxy means $A^{-1}=\sum_{x\in S}\lambda_xxx^T$ for real coefficients $\lambda_x$, while eutaxy requires strictly positive coefficients.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Given an integral matrix $A$, decide whether it is the Bacher matrix of an equivalence class of forms and construct an associated class when one exists. Also give a fast algorithm that decides from a Bacher matrix whether its equivalence class contains a weakly eutactic form. \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2005/01}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
These definitions are the original report\textquotesingle s fixed-minimum, well-rounded conventions. The source does not quantify the requested fast running time.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record open. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-21.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Recognize matrices encoding these quadratic-form cells, reconstruct a cell class, and test whether it contains a weakly eutactic form.

